WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:01.535
[♪ "Danse Macabre" by
Camille Saint-Saens ♪]

00:00:01.535 --> 00:00:04.304
[Narrator] In 1736, a
young Benjamin Franklin

00:00:04.304 --> 00:00:07.007
established the first
volunteer fire department,

00:00:07.007 --> 00:00:10.644
before America had even
declared independence.

00:00:10.644 --> 00:00:12.346
The first automatic
sprinkler system

00:00:12.346 --> 00:00:14.081
was invented in 1872

00:00:14.081 --> 00:00:15.015
[sprinkler pops]

00:00:15.015 --> 00:00:17.184
and perfected a decade later.

00:00:17.184 --> 00:00:20.053
After 100 years, the
smoke alarm came along,

00:00:20.053 --> 00:00:22.389
shortening the time for
fire detection dramatically.

00:00:22.389 --> 00:00:24.524
[fire alarm beeping]

00:00:24.524 --> 00:00:26.360
Humans have created
measures like these

00:00:26.360 --> 00:00:29.096
to protect those things that
are most precious to us:

00:00:29.096 --> 00:00:30.030
our homes,

00:00:30.030 --> 00:00:30.864
valuables,

00:00:30.864 --> 00:00:32.032
vital documents,

00:00:32.032 --> 00:00:33.333
irreplaceable relics,

00:00:33.333 --> 00:00:35.235
priceless memories.

00:00:35.235 --> 00:00:37.871
But today, many of our
most precious items

00:00:37.871 --> 00:00:39.039
have moved online.

00:00:41.408 --> 00:00:44.011
So what do you do to
stop digital fires?

00:00:44.011 --> 00:00:47.080
The code, viruses, malware
and suspicious links

00:00:47.080 --> 00:00:49.082
sent by arsonists that
ignite with a click?

00:00:51.551 --> 00:00:53.720
You do what you've always done.

00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:55.355
You call in the experts—

00:00:55.355 --> 00:00:58.125
the people trained in
putting digital fires out.

00:01:03.864 --> 00:01:04.631
[♪ anthemic music ♪]

00:01:04.631 --> 00:01:07.768
When it's your job to keep
billions of people safe online,

00:01:08.902 --> 00:01:10.537
you have to live and breathe

00:01:10.537 --> 00:01:13.140
and see the internet just
like the attackers do,

00:01:14.308 --> 00:01:16.977
because the only
way to stop a hacker

00:01:17.411 --> 00:01:19.112
is to think like one.

00:01:38.332 --> 00:01:40.267 align:right size:35% position:95%,middle
[Matt] I think if you were
to ask any of my coworkers

00:01:40.267 --> 00:01:41.935 align:right size:35% position:95%,middle
they would probably
tell you that I use

00:01:41.935 --> 00:01:44.438 align:right size:35% position:95%,middle
fire department analogies
a little overbearingly.

00:01:44.438 --> 00:01:45.939
[Narrator] Meet Matt Linton.

00:01:45.939 --> 00:01:48.742
He heads up a security
response team at Google.

00:01:48.742 --> 00:01:50.444
If you asked him
what his team does,

00:01:50.444 --> 00:01:52.679
he often starts at
the very beginning.

00:01:52.679 --> 00:01:53.413
[Matt] When I was a kid,

00:01:53.413 --> 00:01:55.849
I wanted nothing to do
with computers as a career.

00:01:55.849 --> 00:01:58.618
I had a laser focus
on emergency response.

00:01:58.618 --> 00:02:00.988
I got my EMT license at 16,

00:02:00.988 --> 00:02:03.623
technically, before I
got my driver's license.

00:02:04.591 --> 00:02:05.592
[sunglass chime]

00:02:05.592 --> 00:02:08.028
From there I got my
firefighting certification,

00:02:08.028 --> 00:02:10.197
my hazardous
material certification.

00:02:10.197 --> 00:02:11.365
And I just kept going.

00:02:15.168 --> 00:02:16.636
[Narrator] Before
coming to Google,

00:02:16.636 --> 00:02:18.038
Matt clocked over a decade

00:02:18.038 --> 00:02:20.640
conducting specialized
rescue missions,

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:22.709
responding to
hazardous disasters

00:02:22.709 --> 00:02:24.945
and fighting fires at NASA.

00:02:25.345 --> 00:02:27.014
Yep... NASA.

00:02:28.281 --> 00:02:31.284
But what does firefighting
have to do with cybersecurity?

00:02:31.284 --> 00:02:32.819
[Matt] A way that
security at Google

00:02:32.819 --> 00:02:35.489
is an awful lot like
fire department response...

00:02:35.489 --> 00:02:36.490
You have a
fire department

00:02:36.490 --> 00:02:38.125
because even though
we have

00:02:38.492 --> 00:02:39.826
fire sprinklers,

00:02:39.826 --> 00:02:40.994
fire alarms,

00:02:40.994 --> 00:02:41.862
fire inspections

00:02:41.862 --> 00:02:43.730
to prevent fires
from breaking out,

00:02:43.730 --> 00:02:47.134
eventually protections fail
and people make mistakes.

00:02:47.134 --> 00:02:49.336
You need people whose
expertise and specialty

00:02:49.336 --> 00:02:51.838
is in responding to bad
things once they've happened,

00:02:51.838 --> 00:02:53.840
specifically dealing
with the problem at hand

00:02:53.840 --> 00:02:55.575
and not causing a lot
of collateral damage.

00:02:55.976 --> 00:02:57.911
[Narrator] Just as communities
have fire departments

00:02:57.911 --> 00:03:00.580
to protect those things that
are most precious to us,

00:03:00.580 --> 00:03:03.350
Google has its own set
of safety measures—

00:03:03.350 --> 00:03:06.019
the emergency responders,
systems and people

00:03:06.019 --> 00:03:07.687
who are responsible
for protecting parts

00:03:07.687 --> 00:03:09.723
of our digital lives.

00:03:09.723 --> 00:03:11.725
They protect our
childhood photos,

00:03:11.925 --> 00:03:12.826
cherished memories

00:03:12.826 --> 00:03:14.061
and family recipes,

00:03:14.327 --> 00:03:15.395
big ideas,

00:03:15.595 --> 00:03:17.330
blueprints
and business plans,

00:03:17.330 --> 00:03:18.031
finances,

00:03:18.031 --> 00:03:19.900
customer lists and inventories,

00:03:20.567 --> 00:03:21.435
math homework,

00:03:21.835 --> 00:03:22.869
dissertations,

00:03:23.437 --> 00:03:25.439
that one thing you
want no one seeing

00:03:26.239 --> 00:03:28.341
and the billions of things
people and businesses

00:03:28.341 --> 00:03:31.011
use Google for every day.

00:03:31.011 --> 00:03:32.179
[Boy] Happy New Year!

00:03:32.179 --> 00:03:33.713
[Narrator] Now, how
does someone begin

00:03:33.713 --> 00:03:36.416
to handle the pressure
of defending all of that?

00:03:37.684 --> 00:03:42.022 align:right size:35% position:95%,middle
[Royal] I started
cultivating bonsai trees.

00:03:42.522 --> 00:03:44.191
[Narrator] This is Royal Hansen.

00:03:44.191 --> 00:03:47.227
When he is not calming his
nerves with the art of bonsai,

00:03:47.227 --> 00:03:48.462
he's running the entire

00:03:48.462 --> 00:03:51.565
 Privacy, Safety and Security
Team at Google.

00:03:51.932 --> 00:03:53.633
Royal and his team
are responsible

00:03:53.633 --> 00:03:56.470
for the security of
every single Google user,

00:03:56.470 --> 00:03:58.371
which is something he
doesn't take lightly.

00:03:59.206 --> 00:04:02.809
[Royal] Your interaction
with YouTube, Maps,

00:04:02.809 --> 00:04:07.114
websites and companies, it's
literally the way you know

00:04:07.114 --> 00:04:09.583
and live your life
day in and day out.

00:04:09.583 --> 00:04:12.752
There's an enormous
responsibility on us

00:04:13.386 --> 00:04:15.288
to protect user data,

00:04:15.288 --> 00:04:17.090
keep it private.

00:04:17.090 --> 00:04:19.759
And keeping it private
means keeping it safe.

00:04:19.759 --> 00:04:21.895
[Narrator] And to keep
all that information safe

00:04:21.895 --> 00:04:24.431
you need more than an
ordinary fire department.

00:04:24.431 --> 00:04:26.066
Because, well—

00:04:26.066 --> 00:04:28.468 align:right size:35% position:95%,middle
[Fatima] Right now there
are thousands of attacks

00:04:28.468 --> 00:04:31.138 align:right size:35% position:95%,middle
being launched against Google
and against Google customers.

00:04:31.605 --> 00:04:33.140
[Narrator] This
is Fatima Rivera.

00:04:33.140 --> 00:04:34.808
She leads a team
that specializes

00:04:34.808 --> 00:04:36.643
in detecting all those attacks.

00:04:37.244 --> 00:04:39.346
Every single one of them.

00:04:39.346 --> 00:04:41.982
Each with the potential
to start a fire.

00:04:41.982 --> 00:04:43.350
[Fatima] There is no break.

00:04:43.350 --> 00:04:45.752
These attackers that
are all over the world

00:04:45.752 --> 00:04:47.287
are highly skilled.

00:04:47.287 --> 00:04:48.922
It's their job

00:04:48.922 --> 00:04:52.259
24/7 to try to break
into places like Google.

00:04:53.260 --> 00:04:58.265
[Tim] Google is a target of
attack by literally everybody.

00:04:58.965 --> 00:05:01.034 align:right size:35% position:95%,middle
[Narrator] You probably
remember Tim Nguyen

00:05:01.034 --> 00:05:02.936 align:right size:35% position:95%,middle
[Tim voiceover] Hi, this
is Tim. How can I help?

00:05:02.936 --> 00:05:05.872 align:right size:35% position:95%,middle
[Narrator] He may run one of
Google's detection teams now

00:05:05.872 --> 00:05:07.440
but he first got his feet wet

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:09.209
during the single
greatest digital fire

00:05:09.209 --> 00:05:11.845
ever to hit Google:
Operation Aurora.

00:05:11.845 --> 00:05:14.014
[Tim] All you really
need is one hook,

00:05:14.014 --> 00:05:15.782
and you could do
some real damage.

00:05:18.118 --> 00:05:20.654
[Narrator] And in January 2021,

00:05:20.654 --> 00:05:22.022
a group of digital arsonists

00:05:22.022 --> 00:05:24.624
started shooting sparks
across the internet

00:05:24.624 --> 00:05:26.626
trying to get a fire to ignite.

00:05:26.626 --> 00:05:29.296 align:right size:35% position:95%,middle
The Threat Analysis Group
was watching closely.

00:05:29.296 --> 00:05:31.464 align:right size:35% position:95%,middle
[Shane] We started
seeing North Korea

00:05:31.464 --> 00:05:34.501 align:right size:35% position:95%,middle
who instead of targeting
their normal targets,

00:05:34.501 --> 00:05:38.238
which is generally, governments
and cryptocurrency,

00:05:38.238 --> 00:05:41.808
they were starting to directly
trick security researchers.

00:05:41.808 --> 00:05:44.844
[Narrator] Out of all the
possible targets to attack

00:05:44.844 --> 00:05:47.747
the North Korean government
took aim at the very people

00:05:47.747 --> 00:05:50.684
trying to keep the internet
safe from attackers.

00:05:50.684 --> 00:05:52.319
[♪ "Trust in Me"
by Etta James ♪]

00:05:52.319 --> 00:05:55.088
So how do you hack an expert?

00:05:55.855 --> 00:05:58.658
Well, you start with
a convincing name.

00:05:58.658 --> 00:06:00.360
Nothing too generic.

00:06:01.761 --> 00:06:02.362
Ugh...

00:06:02.362 --> 00:06:03.763
too obscure.

00:06:04.097 --> 00:06:05.732
Yeah. Carter.

00:06:06.866 --> 00:06:08.668
Now, what does Carter look like?

00:06:10.604 --> 00:06:13.340
There. That could be a Carter.

00:06:13.340 --> 00:06:17.043
♪ Why don't you,
you trust me? ♪

00:06:17.043 --> 00:06:18.712
[Narrator] Hmm…
Let’s put him at DEF CON.

00:06:18.712 --> 00:06:23.717
♪ Oh, we can get along,
we can get along ♪

00:06:24.451 --> 00:06:27.354
♪ if only you trust in me ♪

00:06:27.354 --> 00:06:28.355
[Narrator] And
there you have it.

00:06:29.556 --> 00:06:31.558
Meet Carter Edwards.

00:06:31.558 --> 00:06:34.628
[Shane] They created fake
social media profiles,

00:06:34.628 --> 00:06:36.062
some websites,

00:06:36.062 --> 00:06:38.098
they created what
looked like a blog.

00:06:38.098 --> 00:06:41.768
♪ Why don't you trust in me? ♪

00:06:41.768 --> 00:06:43.703
[Shane] Trying to sort of
like build up this reputation,

00:06:43.703 --> 00:06:46.373
so they look like a fellow
security researcher.

00:06:46.373 --> 00:06:47.607
[Narrator] The North
Korean government

00:06:47.607 --> 00:06:50.577
fabricated a security
researcher out of nothing.

00:06:50.877 --> 00:06:53.146
But they didn't stop
at Carter Edwards.

00:06:53.146 --> 00:06:55.815
There was James Willy,
Billy Brown,

00:06:55.815 --> 00:06:58.618
Zhang Guo,
Piper Webster.

00:06:58.618 --> 00:07:00.553
North Korea built an entire army

00:07:00.553 --> 00:07:02.389
of fake security researchers

00:07:02.389 --> 00:07:04.324
and deployed them
across the internet

00:07:04.324 --> 00:07:07.227
to become trusted members
of the security community.

00:07:07.227 --> 00:07:09.796
[Tim] The information security
community is fairly small.

00:07:09.796 --> 00:07:13.233
When you gain some
notoriety in the community,

00:07:13.233 --> 00:07:15.602
it's easier to get that
hook into other people.

00:07:15.969 --> 00:07:17.070
[Shane] When they
were emailing or

00:07:17.070 --> 00:07:18.605
contacting these researchers,

00:07:18.605 --> 00:07:20.940
they'd start this
conversation and they'd say,

00:07:20.940 --> 00:07:23.777
"Hey, come check out this
great exploit I found.

00:07:23.777 --> 00:07:24.944
Can you help me look at it?"

00:07:24.944 --> 00:07:28.748
[distorted hissing]

00:07:28.748 --> 00:07:30.750 align:right size:35% position:95%,middle
[Toni] And then what
they would try and do...

00:07:30.750 --> 00:07:32.319 align:right size:35% position:95%,middle
establish a rapport,

00:07:32.319 --> 00:07:34.321
talk about what they
were working on.

00:07:34.321 --> 00:07:37.190
[Narrator] But this wasn't
just water cooler small talk.

00:07:37.190 --> 00:07:38.925
These were calculated
conversations

00:07:38.925 --> 00:07:40.360
about obscure topics

00:07:40.360 --> 00:07:43.096
that only security
researchers care about.

00:07:43.496 --> 00:07:45.365 align:right size:35% position:95%,middle
[Heather] Tricking
security researchers

00:07:45.365 --> 00:07:47.100 align:right size:35% position:95%,middle
into visiting their website,

00:07:47.100 --> 00:07:48.968
and that website would
have an exploit on it.

00:07:48.968 --> 00:07:49.936
And that would give them access

00:07:49.936 --> 00:07:51.438
to the researcher's machine.

00:07:51.438 --> 00:07:53.773
Guess what's on the
researcher's machine?

00:07:53.773 --> 00:07:55.809
Really awesome
security research.

00:07:55.809 --> 00:07:58.678
[Narrator] That research was
highly coveted information,

00:07:58.678 --> 00:08:02.048
like vulnerability libraries
and indexes of exploits.

00:08:02.048 --> 00:08:03.316
They were trying to find out

00:08:03.316 --> 00:08:05.719
how the locksmiths
made their locks.

00:08:05.719 --> 00:08:08.121
And how they could break them.

00:08:08.121 --> 00:08:09.656
[Toni] And one of
the last people

00:08:09.656 --> 00:08:11.958
you want having their hands
on this kind of information

00:08:11.958 --> 00:08:13.860
is the North Korean government.

00:08:14.294 --> 00:08:16.629
[Narrator] If a security
researcher was tricked,

00:08:16.629 --> 00:08:19.866
North Korea could gain
unfettered access to the tools

00:08:19.866 --> 00:08:22.569
needed to steal
user data, passwords

00:08:22.569 --> 00:08:24.471
and personal information.

00:08:24.471 --> 00:08:26.539
They could cause real damage.

00:08:27.841 --> 00:08:30.043
[Shane] North Korea as
an actor is really scary,

00:08:30.043 --> 00:08:32.846
because they have pretty
good capabilities,

00:08:32.846 --> 00:08:35.482
but they have no restraint
in how they use it.

00:08:35.482 --> 00:08:36.983
[Narrator] So when the
Threat Analysis Group

00:08:36.983 --> 00:08:40.520
noticed security researchers
at Google might be a target,

00:08:40.520 --> 00:08:41.988
they had to investigate.

00:08:41.988 --> 00:08:45.158
[Tim] I think we all
took a pause and we knew

00:08:45.158 --> 00:08:47.160
we really have to
answer that question of

00:08:47.160 --> 00:08:48.762
is Google affected by this?

00:08:49.429 --> 00:08:51.097
[Matt] When we
investigated further,

00:08:51.097 --> 00:08:54.167
we found attacks against
Google's security researchers.

00:08:54.167 --> 00:08:55.568
[Narrator] The North
Korean attackers

00:08:55.568 --> 00:08:57.737
had been messaging
Google researchers,

00:08:57.737 --> 00:09:00.507
and some even started
to gain their trust.

00:09:00.507 --> 00:09:02.208
[Matt] We had a conversation
with the Googlers

00:09:02.208 --> 00:09:04.444
who potentially interacted
with the attackers.

00:09:04.444 --> 00:09:06.346
We said, "Hey, have
you seen this person?"

00:09:06.346 --> 00:09:07.781
And we held up the milk jug.

00:09:07.781 --> 00:09:09.449
[Director] Have you
talked to Piper Webster?

00:09:09.449 --> 00:09:11.184
[Heather] Piper Webster?
I don't know.

00:09:11.184 --> 00:09:12.285
[Director] How about
Zhang Guo?

00:09:12.519 --> 00:09:12.886
[Matt] Nope.

00:09:12.886 --> 00:09:13.686
[Director] James Willy?

00:09:13.686 --> 00:09:14.687
[Heather] I don't
know James Willy.

00:09:14.687 --> 00:09:15.622
[Director] Billy Brown?

00:09:15.622 --> 00:09:16.189
[Matt] Nope.

00:09:16.189 --> 00:09:16.923
[Narrator] But then—

00:09:16.923 --> 00:09:18.725
[Matt] Yes, they had
been interacting.

00:09:18.725 --> 00:09:20.193
[Narrator] A Google
employee confirmed

00:09:20.193 --> 00:09:22.462
they'd been messaging
one of the attackers.

00:09:22.862 --> 00:09:24.464
Carter Edwards.

00:09:24.464 --> 00:09:24.898
[wink chime]

00:09:25.565 --> 00:09:28.268
That's the spark. It's
time to sound the alarm.

00:09:28.268 --> 00:09:29.235
[fire alarm rings]

00:09:29.235 --> 00:09:30.737
[Matt] I view us
as a lot like

00:09:30.737 --> 00:09:31.938
the 911 system.
[♪ anthemic music ♪]

00:09:31.938 --> 00:09:35.041
We can be a full 24-hour,
7 day a week

00:09:35.041 --> 00:09:36.576
massive force

00:09:36.576 --> 00:09:38.278
against whatever it
is that we're facing.

00:09:38.278 --> 00:09:39.879
[Narrator] Their first move,

00:09:39.879 --> 00:09:40.947
analyze the breach

00:09:40.947 --> 00:09:44.050
to understand what kind of
fire they're dealing with.

00:09:44.050 --> 00:09:46.052
[Matt] We do a complete
forensic analysis

00:09:46.052 --> 00:09:47.287
on the workstation
of the Googler,

00:09:47.754 --> 00:09:49.622
we have people that
understand attack code.

00:09:49.622 --> 00:09:51.090
They reverse it.

00:09:51.090 --> 00:09:54.394
They provide us with signals
that we can look for elsewhere.

00:09:54.394 --> 00:09:57.096
Who on the internet does
this malicious code talk to?

00:09:57.096 --> 00:09:59.065
What domain names does it use?

00:09:59.065 --> 00:10:01.901
What does it do to the system
while it's embedding itself?

00:10:01.901 --> 00:10:03.570
[Narrator] That initial
breach allows them

00:10:03.570 --> 00:10:06.573
to identify the mechanics
of the North Korean attack.

00:10:07.273 --> 00:10:09.476
But is this breach the only one?

00:10:09.476 --> 00:10:12.579
Or have the North Korean
attackers found another way in?

00:10:15.949 --> 00:10:18.418
[♪ soft music ♪]

00:10:19.519 --> 00:10:21.521 align:right size:35% position:95%,middle
This is Alex Stamos.

00:10:21.521 --> 00:10:24.624 align:right size:35% position:95%,middle
He's the director of the
Stanford Internet Observatory,

00:10:24.624 --> 00:10:28.194
a research program devoted
to studying safety online.

00:10:28.194 --> 00:10:30.430
Knowing the scope of
Google’s cybersecurity

00:10:30.430 --> 00:10:31.764
comes with a job.

00:10:31.764 --> 00:10:33.633
[Alex] When you're defending
millions of computers

00:10:33.633 --> 00:10:35.702
from tens of
thousands of attackers

00:10:35.702 --> 00:10:39.138
you have to have a huge amount
of data at your fingertips,

00:10:39.138 --> 00:10:40.940
but then also the ability
to sift through it

00:10:40.940 --> 00:10:42.308
really, really quickly.

00:10:42.308 --> 00:10:43.943
Google's Detection and
Response Team

00:10:43.943 --> 00:10:45.345
has been at the forefront

00:10:45.345 --> 00:10:47.780
of using big data tooling

00:10:47.780 --> 00:10:49.782
to gather up petabytes
and petabytes

00:10:49.782 --> 00:10:51.284
of security relevant data

00:10:51.284 --> 00:10:53.553
and to very quickly
look for anomalies.

00:10:53.987 --> 00:10:55.822
[Matt] Once we have indicators

00:10:55.822 --> 00:10:57.957
from that one laptop
we've analyzed—

00:10:57.957 --> 00:10:59.526
[Tim] We run what's
called the fleet check,

00:10:59.526 --> 00:11:02.829
which is, checking literally
every single machine

00:11:02.829 --> 00:11:04.797
for these types of indicators.

00:11:04.797 --> 00:11:05.798
[Narrator] Every.

00:11:05.798 --> 00:11:06.833
Single.

00:11:06.833 --> 00:11:08.301
Google machine.

00:11:08.301 --> 00:11:10.203
Every server and every computer

00:11:10.203 --> 00:11:12.171
on Google's internal network.

00:11:12.171 --> 00:11:13.406
[Matt] We find
any other computer

00:11:13.406 --> 00:11:15.742
that has downloaded
the same package,

00:11:15.742 --> 00:11:17.510
executed something
similar to it,

00:11:17.510 --> 00:11:19.379
executed something
from the same person,

00:11:19.379 --> 00:11:21.981
and all of the ways that it
possibly could have made its way

00:11:21.981 --> 00:11:23.883
into Google, we can
look for all of those.

00:11:24.317 --> 00:11:25.518
[Narrator] They take
all the evidence

00:11:25.518 --> 00:11:27.086
from the initial breach,

00:11:27.086 --> 00:11:29.989
the code signatures, domain
names and IP addresses.

00:11:29.989 --> 00:11:30.857
[indistinct talking]

00:11:30.857 --> 00:11:32.759
And run them through
the automated systems

00:11:32.759 --> 00:11:35.061
that scan the entire
Google network.

00:11:35.662 --> 00:11:36.596
[Tim] From the billions of logs

00:11:36.596 --> 00:11:37.764
we'll start to pattern match

00:11:37.764 --> 00:11:40.700
against our many
thousands of signals.

00:11:40.700 --> 00:11:44.404
It actually really boils down
to a very small number

00:11:44.404 --> 00:11:45.705
of what we call true positives,

00:11:45.705 --> 00:11:48.408
and that's where we see
something bad happening.

00:11:48.408 --> 00:11:51.344
And from there a human
being will start to analyze

00:11:51.344 --> 00:11:52.412
and dig deeper.

00:11:52.779 --> 00:11:55.415
[Matt] Watching analysts
build a cohesive picture

00:11:55.415 --> 00:11:57.383
of what happened and
when, it's like watching

00:11:57.383 --> 00:11:59.586
one of those professional
Rubik's Cube solvers at work.

00:12:00.520 --> 00:12:01.888
It's just this
jumble of garbage,

00:12:01.888 --> 00:12:03.856
and then these nimble
hands are working,

00:12:03.856 --> 00:12:06.359
and then suddenly it's clear.

00:12:06.359 --> 00:12:08.294
There's this clear
picture of what happened.

00:12:08.962 --> 00:12:11.864
They were gaining a foothold
on a Googler's machine.

00:12:11.864 --> 00:12:14.367
We actually do
find malicious code

00:12:14.367 --> 00:12:16.436
that the attacker has been
serving to the Googler.

00:12:17.770 --> 00:12:19.772
[Fatima] We assume the
worst case scenario.

00:12:19.772 --> 00:12:22.308
So there's definitely
that moment of panic

00:12:22.308 --> 00:12:23.476
where you just don't know.

00:12:23.476 --> 00:12:24.978
It's all the unknown.

00:12:25.378 --> 00:12:28.247
But you have to
reel yourself in.

00:12:28.247 --> 00:12:32.085
You really cannot think properly
and take the right steps

00:12:32.085 --> 00:12:33.286
if you're panicked.

00:12:33.286 --> 00:12:34.988
[Narrator] Sparks are
starting to catch.

00:12:34.988 --> 00:12:36.155
The North Korean government

00:12:36.155 --> 00:12:38.625
has compromised
a second machine.

00:12:38.625 --> 00:12:41.060
These hackers are
fast and precise.

00:12:41.327 --> 00:12:43.596
If they get further
inside Google,

00:12:43.596 --> 00:12:45.131
who knows what they could do.

00:12:45.732 --> 00:12:48.101
It's time to start
putting this fire out.

00:12:48.101 --> 00:12:49.235
[Commentator] This
calls for precision

00:12:49.235 --> 00:12:50.603
and fire fighting teamwork.

00:12:50.603 --> 00:12:52.672
Wasted seconds
could prove fatal.

00:12:52.672 --> 00:12:56.943
[Tim] The next step is to
hunt for where the attacker

00:12:56.943 --> 00:12:58.678
may still be on the network.

00:12:58.678 --> 00:13:01.147
Part of that is not
letting the attacker know

00:13:01.147 --> 00:13:02.148
that you're onto them.

00:13:02.148 --> 00:13:03.683
The one thing we don't do

00:13:03.683 --> 00:13:06.085
is we don't start to shut
down machines right away.

00:13:06.085 --> 00:13:07.587
[Fatima] We don't
want them to pivot.

00:13:07.587 --> 00:13:09.856
We don't want them to use a
different attack technique

00:13:09.856 --> 00:13:11.024
than what we know.

00:13:11.024 --> 00:13:13.192
So the goal when we're
hunting them down

00:13:13.192 --> 00:13:17.030
is that we're keeping up
pace and really luring them

00:13:17.030 --> 00:13:20.633
into a position that
allows us to block them out

00:13:20.633 --> 00:13:21.634
in one step.

00:13:21.968 --> 00:13:24.170
[Matt] Once we're certain
about where they've been,

00:13:24.170 --> 00:13:26.372
that's when we
start to act in ways

00:13:26.372 --> 00:13:28.007
that are visible
to the attacker.

00:13:30.009 --> 00:13:31.511
[Fatima] We're
gonna box them in.

00:13:31.511 --> 00:13:32.679
[Tim] We'll start to try

00:13:32.679 --> 00:13:34.714
and cut off their
connections. And then—

00:13:34.714 --> 00:13:37.216
[Matt] We yank the rug out
from them all at once,

00:13:37.216 --> 00:13:40.353
all within the same
short period of time

00:13:40.353 --> 00:13:42.355
by a group of people
acting in concert.

00:13:42.355 --> 00:13:44.724
[Narrator] They patch every
hole the attackers have made.

00:13:44.724 --> 00:13:47.160
Sometimes that's all it
takes to seal them off.

00:13:47.160 --> 00:13:49.128
But in the case of
an advanced threat,

00:13:49.128 --> 00:13:50.797
like the North
Korean government—

00:13:50.797 --> 00:13:52.632
[Tim] The only way to assure

00:13:52.632 --> 00:13:54.634
that a threat does
not exist anymore

00:13:54.634 --> 00:13:58.838
is by actually having a fresh
installation on that machine.

00:13:58.838 --> 00:14:00.640
[Fatima] We have to burn
it all down to the ground

00:14:00.640 --> 00:14:02.308
and start from scratch.

00:14:02.308 --> 00:14:03.309
[Matt] Burn it down.

00:14:03.309 --> 00:14:05.511
Why take the risk that
you missed a back door?

00:14:05.511 --> 00:14:06.813
[Tim] I don't like
burning things down

00:14:06.813 --> 00:14:08.481
because it's really disruptive.

00:14:08.481 --> 00:14:09.916
It's a bad day for everybody.

00:14:09.916 --> 00:14:12.218
[Matt] I love it. [laughs]

00:14:12.218 --> 00:14:13.553
[explosion booms]

00:14:13.553 --> 00:14:14.854
[explosion booms]
[laughs]

00:14:14.854 --> 00:14:16.189
[Narrator] And just like that,

00:14:16.189 --> 00:14:19.192
the North Korean attackers
were kicked out of Google.

00:14:19.192 --> 00:14:21.761
A campaign that waged
for weeks was foiled

00:14:21.761 --> 00:14:25.031
by the Detection and Response
Team in just a few hours.

00:14:25.331 --> 00:14:27.500
Only two computers
were compromised.

00:14:27.500 --> 00:14:30.336
No users were affected,
and thankfully—

00:14:30.336 --> 00:14:32.872
[Fatima] They weren't
able to cause any damage.

00:14:32.872 --> 00:14:35.108
That's a good moment when
you realize that everything

00:14:35.108 --> 00:14:37.744
that you have put in
place, all the hard work,

00:14:37.744 --> 00:14:38.845
that it just pays off.

00:14:39.345 --> 00:14:41.114
[Narrator] And to
really seal the deal—

00:14:41.114 --> 00:14:43.116
[Matt] We were able to
make sure we take the tools

00:14:43.116 --> 00:14:43.783
and the indicators

00:14:43.783 --> 00:14:45.518
and roll them back into
our detection pipeline.

00:14:45.718 --> 00:14:47.286
[Narrator] Every
piece of evidence,

00:14:47.286 --> 00:14:49.922
the URLs, domain names
and code signatures

00:14:49.922 --> 00:14:51.457
from the North Korean attack

00:14:51.457 --> 00:14:54.460
were loaded back into
Google's detection system.

00:14:54.460 --> 00:14:56.462
With every fire
the team puts out

00:14:56.462 --> 00:14:58.865
Google's defenses
become stronger.

00:14:58.865 --> 00:15:01.234
[Alex] The difference
between a company that can

00:15:01.234 --> 00:15:02.201
protect itself

00:15:02.201 --> 00:15:03.569
versus the ones that can't,

00:15:03.569 --> 00:15:07.073
is having a team that
is constantly responding

00:15:07.073 --> 00:15:09.976
and assuming that the
attackers are on the inside

00:15:09.976 --> 00:15:12.612
instead of just building
a hard outside shell

00:15:12.612 --> 00:15:14.113
and pretending that
that's good enough

00:15:14.113 --> 00:15:15.882
to keep people from getting in.

00:15:15.882 --> 00:15:18.484
[Narrator] But these responders
don't just answer the call

00:15:18.484 --> 00:15:21.154
when Google's own
walls are threatened.

00:15:21.154 --> 00:15:22.722
The teams and
sophisticated tools

00:15:22.722 --> 00:15:25.258
protecting against the
North Korean attackers

00:15:25.258 --> 00:15:28.327
are the same ones protecting
you and your data.

00:15:28.327 --> 00:15:30.663
[Royal] Every year the
amount of data created,

00:15:30.663 --> 00:15:32.398
the amount of digital journeys

00:15:32.398 --> 00:15:34.167
that a consumer will
have on the internet

00:15:34.167 --> 00:15:36.969
have just exploded, and with it

00:15:36.969 --> 00:15:38.437
the opportunity for hacking.

00:15:38.771 --> 00:15:41.440
[Tim] The North Korea attack,
it really reiterated to me

00:15:41.440 --> 00:15:43.075
the importance of our mission.

00:15:43.075 --> 00:15:46.445
If you interact with
Google in any way,

00:15:46.445 --> 00:15:50.283
we are here to keep your
information, your data safe

00:15:50.283 --> 00:15:53.653
from those that would
sometimes wish to do you harm.

00:15:54.854 --> 00:15:56.689
[Matt] We really do
care about your data

00:15:56.689 --> 00:15:58.357
being protected so much

00:15:58.357 --> 00:16:00.092
that we have the fire department

00:16:00.092 --> 00:16:02.094
for your systems and your data.

00:16:02.094 --> 00:16:04.130
And we're always on duty.

00:16:09.969 --> 00:16:12.305
[♪ anthemic music ♪]

00:16:12.305 --> 00:16:14.707
[Daniel] My team likes
to set stuff on fire.

00:16:14.707 --> 00:16:16.309
[Heather] The Red Team
are my favorite enemies.

00:16:16.309 --> 00:16:19.011
[Daniel] We basically take
the role of an attacker

00:16:19.011 --> 00:16:20.446
and hack into Google.

00:16:20.446 --> 00:16:22.215
[Niru] The car companies
crash their cars

00:16:22.215 --> 00:16:23.516
to make sure they're safe.

00:16:23.883 --> 00:16:24.617
So do we.

00:16:24.617 --> 00:16:25.685
[Daniel] That you would see for

00:16:25.685 --> 00:16:27.320
like about a 10th of a second

00:16:27.320 --> 00:16:29.555
a black window flash
up on the screen

00:16:29.555 --> 00:16:31.224
[beep]
and that is that.

00:16:31.224 --> 00:16:33.192
[Niru] Yes, we are in! [laughs]
